7.1. Key findings:
(1) END was frequent (1 in 4 in the first 24h, 1 in 3 within the first week), predominantly in case of nonatheromatous cause, was generally severe and was strongly associated with poor 3-month outcome; 
(2) END was of thromboembolic origin in two-thirds of the cases; 
(3) the rate of END within the first 24 hours was 2-fold higher following IVT (33% vs 16%, adjusted HR 2.01, P=0.03), driven by higher odds of thromboembolic END;
(4) however, the rate of END occurring within 7 days did not significantly differ between the 2 treatment groups  (37% vs 28%, P=0.37), and 3-month outcome was similar.
Rescue EVT improved outcomes in END patients.
